<Step id="STEP_2_IMPLEMENT">
      <Action>
        Implement the smallest change satisfying the phase and its referenced requirements. Stay
        inside target files. Do not alter task dependencies, phases, approved BDD/test plan, or spec
        meaning. If such a change is necessary, return `APPROVAL_BOUNDARY_CHANGE` with evidence.
        Create and preserve V2 file headers per `AX_FILE_HEADER_TASK_TRACEABILITY` and
        `AX_FILE_HEADER_APPEND_ONLY`: semantic ownership is `@spec`; never copy ticket history into
        the source header.
        The phase protocol defines the permitted command list per `AX_PERMITTED_BASH_COMMANDS`; every
        bash invocation while implementing stays within it.
        Record real progress as it happens — one `PHASE_BLOCK_FORMAT` event line per
        intro/decision/tried/discovery/insight, never batched from memory afterward:
        <ToolCall owner="phase-agent" result="terminal">npx gennady sdd-log &lt;ticket&gt; line "&lt;event&gt;" --phase &lt;PhaseID&gt;</ToolCall>
        Inputs &lt;ticket&gt;, &lt;event&gt;, &lt;PhaseID&gt;: this phase's own ticket path, the exact
        event line per its token's grammar, this phase's id.
        When stuck (`AX_BLOCKER_ESCALATION`'s triggers — a missing rule file, a broken Handoff
        promise, a self-contradicting spec, a Target Files boundary, an unhonorable Runtime
        Backing, a verification command that cannot run), write the reason/axiom/unblock JSON keys
        with the file-write tool to `&lt;payload-path&gt;`, then open `BLOCKER_FORMAT` and stop:
        <ToolCall owner="phase-agent" result="terminal">npx gennady sdd-log &lt;ticket&gt; blocker --payload-file &lt;payload-path&gt; --phase &lt;PhaseID&gt;</ToolCall>
        Inputs &lt;ticket&gt;, &lt;payload-path&gt;, &lt;PhaseID&gt;: this phase's own ticket path, the
        just-written `.claude/tmp/<task-id>-<phase-id>-log.json` payload, this phase's id. Once the
        cause is actually gone, close it per `AX_BLOCKER_RESOLUTION_TRAIL` (`sdd-log resolved`,
        never a manual edit).
      </Action>
    </Step>

<Axiom id="AX_BLOCKER_RESOLUTION_TRAIL">
      A BLOCKER entry in Execution Log (line containing `🛑` and `BLOCKED`) remains ACTIVE until
      `## Blocker Trail` carries a matching resolution line — `✅ RESOLVED (Round <N> / <PhaseID>)`
      (D-20, B2-19). The back-reference is what pairs it to the right occurrence; a resolution is
      NEVER appended inline in the Execution Log itself, so it works whether or not that Round has
      already closed.

      Orchestrator consumes the `[BLOCKERS]` section already returned by `sdd-task <ticket>`.
      `blockers: none` is clear; each `blockers: ACTIVE <n>` entry is classified through the typed
      checkpoint as recoverable technical, spec-goal conflict, or external authority. Active history
      never implies an operator pause by itself. Do not spend a second check call or reconstruct it.

      Resolution authorship: the `## Blocker Trail` entry MUST cite the specific environmental
      change or decision that resolved the blocker (e.g. "tsconfig moduleResolution fixed",
      "operator chose option B"). Empty resolution text → audit treats as fabricated and may re-flag.

      Append-only discipline: never edit or remove the original `🛑 BLOCKED` line, and never hand-edit
      `## Blocker Trail` — only `npx gennady sdd-log <ticket> resolved` writes it. The blocker
      history is part of the artifact.
    </Axiom>

<Axiom id="AX_FILE_HEADER_TASK_TRACEABILITY">
      **V2 files carry one semantic owner; task relations come from the deterministic resolver.**

      Required file-level fields in execution profile:
      - `@file` — mandatory; what the file holds.
      - `@spec` — mandatory; exactly one stable canonical Spec ID, never a path.
      - `@consumers` — mandatory; one or more named consumers from the owning spec.

      `@tasks` is legacy V1 migration evidence and is forbidden in a new or migrated V2 header.
      `planned`, `active`, `blocked`, and `history` task relations are resolved from exact ticket
      targets and lifecycle state; none is copied into the header. Only an `active` relation may
      continue/recover under task state policy. `history` is context, never routing authority.

      Canonical header shape:
      ```
      // @file: <what the file holds>
      // @spec: <CANONICAL-SPEC-ID>
      // @consumers: <consumer-1, consumer-2, ...>
      ```
    </Axiom>

<Axiom id="AX_FILE_HEADER_APPEND_ONLY">
      Touching a V2 file preserves its semantic-owner header; task history stays in tickets and Git.

      - Required V2 fields, in order: `@file:`, one canonical `@spec:` ID, `@consumers:`.
      - New V2 file: create all three from the resolved owner spec and its named consumers.
      - Existing V2 file: preserve `@file:` and `@consumers:`; keep `@spec:` on the resolved semantic
        owner. A real ownership transfer is a semantic spec change and routes through authoring.
      - Existing V1 file remains byte-preserved until its migration owns the header rewrite.
      - Never add a Task-ID to a V2 source header; re-dispatch and later rounds change resolver state,
        not header history.
    </Axiom>

<Contract id="PHASE_BLOCK_FORMAT">
  Event-driven: optional event lines (0+ each) appear ONLY when their event happened, opening
  with a token from the CLOSED vocabulary `sdd-check` enforces mechanically
  (`SDD_EXECUTION_LOG_UNKNOWN_TOKEN` on anything else) — never hand-copy that list here, it has
  exactly one home. STEP_5 atomically inserts the paired structured `SDD_PHASE_RECEIPT` block;
  the agent never authors it. Semantic closing lines remain `DONE` + Handoff.

  ```markdown
  #### <PhaseID>                                 <!-- or "#### <PhaseID> — re-run: <reason>" -->
  <!-- Event lines (optional; appended as events happen, in chronological order): -->
  - [x] `<ts>` <token> <per the token's own grammar — e.g. `intro <Entity> ← <reason>`, `decision <key>=<value> ← <reason>`>
  <!-- CLI-owned receipt appears here after successful STEP_5. -->
  <!-- Semantic closing lines (mandatory): -->
  - [x] `<ts>` DONE
  **Handoff →** artifacts: [<paths>]; decisions: [<key=value, …>]; open: [<id: text, …>]; deviations: [<id: text, …>]
  ```

  Trivial phase: one receipt + `DONE` + Handoff. That is valid and preferred over padding.
</Contract>

<Contract id="BLOCKER_FORMAT">
  ```markdown
  - 🛑 `<ts>` BLOCKED: <one-line cause>
    - 🔗 axiom: AX_<NAME>
    - 💬 unblock: <concrete operator action>
  ```
  After this entry: phase Status → `[!] BLOCKED`; return BLOCKED to orchestrator. Stop.

  Paired close, once the cause is actually gone (`AX_BLOCKER_RESOLUTION_TRAIL`): run `npx
  gennady sdd-log <ticket> resolved "<what removed it>" --phase <PhaseID>` — never a manual
  `Edit`, never the original 🛑 line touched. The CLI writes the resolution into `## Blocker
  Trail`, a section of its own outside the Execution Log (D-20) — NOT back into this phase's
  block, and it works even after the Round that raised the blocker has already closed:
  ```markdown
  - [x] `<ts>` ✅ RESOLVED (Round <N> / <PhaseID>): <what removed it>
  ```
</Contract>